<section class="sub" id="ahi-state">
<div class="section-head">
<div class="section-num">01 · STATE-LEVEL PROGRAMS</div>
<h2 class="section-title">What states are doing</h2>
<p class="section-note">Five state programs that use density, tax, or funding carrots — not preemption alone — to move the needle on production.</p>
</div>
<div class="case-study-list">
<article class="card case-study" id="ahs-california">
<div class="card-top">
<h3><a href="/cities/california/">California</a><span class="place">Density bonus &amp; ADU laws</span></h3>
<span class="cs-tag">State program</span>
</div>
<div class="tagline">Gov. Code §65915 · AB 1287 (2024) · ADU law series 2016–23</div>
<span class="field-label">Description</span>
<p>State law gives developers extra height, density, and reduced parking by-right in exchange for including affordable units — no rezoning or hearing required. A parallel decade of state ADU laws made backyard and internal accessory units nearly by-right statewide.</p>
<span class="field-label">Legislation</span>
<ul class="leg-list">
<li><a href="https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?sectionNum=65915&amp;lawCode=GOV">Cal. Gov. Code §65915</a> — the State Density Bonus Law, requiring cities to grant increased density and other concessions to developments that include affordable, senior, or student housing.</li>
<li><a href="https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/billTextClient.xhtml?bill_id=201720180SB35">SB 35 (2017)</a> — created ministerial, streamlined approval — bypassing discretionary review and CEQA — for qualifying infill housing in cities behind their state housing targets.</li>
<li><a href="https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/billNavClient.xhtml?bill_id=202320240SB423">SB 423 (2023)</a> — extended SB 35's streamlined approval past its original 2026 expiration and adjusted its eligibility criteria.</li>
<li><a href="https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/billNavClient.xhtml?bill_id=202320240AB1287">AB 1287 (2023, eff. 2024)</a> — added a further density-bonus tier for developments that include additional very-low- or moderate-income units beyond the base thresholds.</li>
<li><a href="https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/billTextClient.xhtml?bill_id=202120220SB9">SB 9 (2021)</a> — requires ministerial approval of up to two units on a single-family lot, or a lot split into two, subject to conditions.</li>
<li><a href="https://www.hcd.ca.gov/building-standards/adu/handbook">State ADU law series (2016–2023)</a> — a cumulative set of laws that progressively removed local barriers — parking minimums, size caps, owner-occupancy rules, fees, review timelines — to building accessory dwelling units.</li>
</ul>
<span class="field-label">Objective results</span>
<div class="stat-row">
<span class="stat"><b>140,000+</b> homes enabled by the density bonus law, 69,000+ affordable</span>
<span class="stat"><b>429,503</b> ADU permits statewide since 2018 — 32% of all U.S. ADU permits</span>
<span class="stat"><b>18,000+</b> units via SB 35 streamlining before its 2025 sunset (extended by SB 423)</span>
</div>
<span class="field-label">Subjective results</span>
<p class="muted">SB 9's parallel duplex/lot-split law shows the limits of legal permission alone: about 266 permits statewide as of 2023 against 6.1 million eligible parcels — a reminder that legal permission alone doesn't guarantee uptake if the economics don't pencil out for a typical property owner.</p>
<span class="field-label">Public response</span>
<p class="muted">The by-right, no-hearing design has held across three governorships and repeated legislative sessions — SB 35 (2017) was extended via SB 423 (2023) rather than allowed to sunset, and AB 1287 (2024) added a second density-bonus tier on top of the original law. Berkeley's <em>Ruegg &amp; Ellsworth v. City of Berkeley</em> (2021) tested SB 35's override of local objections, including historic-preservation claims, and the city lost — reinforcing rather than weakening the tool's reach.</p>
<span class="field-label">Sources</span>
<div class="src-row"><a href="https://legal-planet.org/2026/06/">Legal Planet, Circulate SD "Win-Win Bonus" report ↗</a>
<a href="https://ternercenter.berkeley.edu/">Terner Center, SB 35 tracking ↗</a>
<a href="https://www.hcd.ca.gov/">California HCD, ADU permit data ↗</a></div>
</article>
<article class="card case-study" id="ahs-massachusetts">
<div class="card-top">
<h3><a href="/cities/massachusetts/">Massachusetts</a><span class="place">Chapter 40B comprehensive permit</span></h3>
<span class="cs-tag">State program</span>
</div>
<div class="tagline">Enacted 1969 · reform proposals active 2025–26</div>
<span class="field-label">Description</span>
<p>In any town below 10% subsidized housing, a developer can bypass local zoning boards entirely through a single comprehensive permit — one hearing instead of six — in exchange for reserving 20–25% of units as long-term affordable. A denial can be appealed to a state board, which can override it.</p>
<span class="field-label">Legislation</span>
<ul class="leg-list">
<li><a href="https://malegislature.gov/Laws/GeneralLaws/PartI/TitleVII/Chapter40B">M.G.L. c. 40B, §§20–23</a> — enacted in 1969, lets developers of qualifying affordable housing apply for a single comprehensive permit from the local zoning board, with a state appeal available if a permit is denied or conditioned into infeasibility.</li>
</ul>
<span class="field-label">Objective results</span>
<div class="stat-row">
<span class="stat"><b>~70,000</b> homes produced statewide since 1969</span>
<span class="stat"><b>35,000+</b> of those income-restricted</span>
</div>
<span class="field-label">Subjective results</span>
<p class="muted">The model has proven exportable as well as durable — Connecticut (§8-30g), Rhode Island, and Illinois all adopted similar override structures. Reform proposals active in 2025–26, including bond requirements for challengers and added incentives for towns already above 12% affordable, show a 55-year-old program still being tuned rather than left alone.</p>
<span class="field-label">Public response</span>
<p class="muted">Massachusetts voters upheld the law directly, rejecting a 2010 statewide ballot measure to repeal it. The state's Supreme Judicial Court narrowed its scope at the margins in 2017 (holding it can waive zoning but not amend municipally-owned deed restrictions) without disturbing the core mechanism — the law has been tested at the ballot box and in court, and has survived both.</p>
<span class="field-label">Sources</span>
<div class="src-row"><a href="https://www.masshousing.com/en/programs-outreach/planning-programs/40b">MassHousing — Chapter 40B overview ↗</a>
<a href="https://www.mass.gov/chapter-40b-planning-and-information">Mass.gov — Chapter 40B planning &amp; information ↗</a></div>
</article>
<article class="card case-study" id="ahs-washington">
<div class="card-top">
<h3><a href="/cities/seattle/">Washington</a><span class="place">Multifamily Tax Exemption (MFTE)</span></h3>
<span class="cs-tag">State program</span>
</div>
<div class="tagline">RCW 84.14 · new transit tier added 2025</div>
<span class="field-label">Description</span>
<p>State law lets cities exempt the residential improvement value of new multifamily buildings from property tax for 8, 12, or 20 years, with longer terms tied to deeper affordability. More than 50 Washington cities now run a local program under this framework.</p>
<span class="field-label">Legislation</span>
<ul class="leg-list">
<li><a href="https://apps.leg.wa.gov/rCW/default.aspx?cite=84.14&amp;full=true">RCW 84.14</a> — the Multifamily Property Tax Exemption statute, letting cities exempt new multifamily buildings' improvement value from property tax for 8, 12, or 20 years.</li>
<li><a href="https://app.leg.wa.gov/billsummary?BillNumber=1491&amp;Year=2025">3SHB 1491 (2025)</a> — added a new, separate 20-year exemption tier for qualifying affordable housing near transit stations; signed as Chapter 267, Laws of 2025.</li>
</ul>
<span class="field-label">Objective results</span>
<div class="stat-row">
<span class="stat"><b>~10,700</b> units produced statewide in 2024, <b>~8,100</b> in 2025</span>
<span class="stat"><b>~30%</b> of all new WA multifamily construction over the last two years</span>
</div>
<span class="field-label">Subjective results</span>
<p class="muted">A 2025 update (3SHB 1491) added a new 20-year tier specifically for housing near transit stations — formally tying the tax break to walkable, low-car-dependence locations, which is exactly the link Santa Fe would need any similar tool to make. See <a href="/research/housing-incentives/#ahc-seattle">Seattle</a>, <a href="/research/housing-incentives/#ahc-tacoma">Tacoma</a>, and <a href="/research/housing-incentives/#ahc-spokane">Spokane</a> below for how the same statewide framework plays out at different city scales.</p>
<span class="field-label">Public response</span>
<p class="muted">The legislature has continued to expand and refine MFTE even as individual cities recalibrate: Seattle's own October 2025 reforms (below) tightened terms after an independent cost-benefit audit, while the state simultaneously added the transit tier the same year — a sign of active, ongoing adjustment rather than a settled program.</p>
<span class="field-label">Sources</span>
<div class="src-row"><a href="https://apps.leg.wa.gov/rCW/default.aspx?cite=84.14&amp;full=true">RCW 84.14, full text ↗</a>
<a href="https://static.spokanecity.org/documents/economicdevelopment/incentives/mfte/2025-mfte-legislative-report.pdf">2025 statewide MFTE legislative report ↗</a></div>
</article>
<article class="card case-study" id="ahs-colorado">
<div class="card-top">
<h3>Colorado<span class="place">Prop 123 &amp; Transit-Oriented Communities grants</span></h3>
<span class="cs-tag">State program</span>
</div>
<div class="tagline">Prop 123 passed 2022 · TOCI pilot round 2026</div>
<span class="field-label">Description</span>
<p>Voters dedicated 0.1% of state income tax revenue — about $300 million a year — to a state affordable-housing fund. Cities and counties access the money by committing to specific housing-growth targets. A newer Transit-Oriented Communities Infrastructure grant funds the streets, sidewalks, and utility work that makes transit-adjacent housing feasible to build.</p>
<span class="field-label">Legislation</span>
<ul class="leg-list">
<li><a href="https://content.leg.colorado.gov/sites/default/files/images/blue_book_2022_english_for_web.pdf">Proposition 123 (2022)</a> — the voter-approved ballot measure dedicating 0.1% of state income tax revenue to affordable housing programs, exempt from the state's constitutional spending cap.</li>
<li><a href="https://leg.colorado.gov/bills/hb24-1313">HB24-1313 (2024), Housing in Transit-Oriented Communities</a> — established the transit-oriented community designation and created the Transit-Oriented Communities Infrastructure Fund, which grants qualifying communities money for infrastructure supporting new housing near transit.</li>
</ul>
<span class="field-label">Objective results</span>
<div class="stat-row">
<span class="stat"><b>200+</b> jurisdictions, over 90% of the state's population, committed to Prop 123</span>
<span class="stat"><b>$13.3M</b> awarded to four cities in the first TOCI grant round (early 2026)</span>
</div>
<span class="field-label">Subjective results</span>
<p class="muted">Unlike California, Massachusetts, or Washington, Colorado's tools are funding-based rather than a zoning override or by-right entitlement — the money only converts to housing where a jurisdiction already has the zoning capacity to build. That's the same pattern Recommendation 6 draws on: funding without buildable capacity, or capacity without funding, both tend to stall.</p>
<span class="field-label">Public response</span>
<p class="muted">Prop 123 passed as a statewide ballot initiative in November 2022 — a rare instance of voters approving a dedicated, ongoing tax-revenue commitment to housing production rather than a one-time bond. No source reviewed confirms whether any specific city in this library's scan, including <a href="/cities/boulder/">Boulder</a>, has opted into Prop 123 or received TOCI funds; that connection is left unconfirmed here rather than assumed.</p>
<span class="field-label">Sources</span>
<div class="src-row"><a href="https://cdola.colorado.gov/about-proposition-123">Colorado DOLA — Proposition 123 overview ↗</a>
<a href="https://dlg.colorado.gov/toci-grant-program">Colorado DOLA — TOCI grant program ↗</a></div>
</article>
<article class="card case-study" id="ahs-minnesota">
<div class="card-top">
<h3><a href="/cities/minneapolis/">Minnesota</a><span class="place">Local Affordable Housing Aid</span></h3>
<span class="cs-tag">State program</span>
</div>
<div class="tagline">Enacted 2023 · Minn. Stat. 477A.35</div>
<span class="field-label">Description</span>
<p>A dedicated sales-tax-funded account for the seven-county Twin Cities metro, distributing money directly to cities and counties for affordable housing construction, rehabilitation, and rental assistance. It complements Minneapolis's own 4(d) property-tax incentive (below) rather than replacing it.</p>
<span class="field-label">Legislation</span>
<ul class="leg-list">
<li><a href="https://www.revisor.mn.gov/statutes/cite/477A.35">Minn. Stat. §477A.35</a> — establishes Local Affordable Housing Aid, a formula-based state aid program distributing funds to counties and larger metro cities for affordable housing construction, rehabilitation, and rental assistance.</li>
</ul>
<span class="field-label">Objective results</span>
<div class="stat-row">
<span class="stat"><b>$48.3M</b> distributed in 2024 alone</span>
</div>
<span class="field-label">Subjective results</span>
<p class="muted">With only one year of distributions on record, it's too early to assess durability the way California's or Massachusetts's multi-decade programs allow. What is measurable is speed: the fund went from statute (2023) to $48.3M disbursed within its first full year, pairing new regional money with a city — Minneapolis — that already had the zoning capacity to put it to use.</p>
<span class="field-label">Public response</span>
<p class="muted">Enacted through ordinary legislation in 2023, not a ballot measure. No independent public-reception survey was located; the fund's design as a standing regional revenue-sharing account, rather than a one-time appropriation, suggests it was built for continuity rather than a single budget cycle.</p>
<span class="field-label">Sources</span>
<div class="src-row"><a href="https://www.revenue.state.mn.us/local-affordable-housing-aid">Minnesota Dept. of Revenue — Local Affordable Housing Aid ↗</a></div>
</article>
</div>
</section>
<section class="sub" id="ahi-city">
<div class="section-head">
<div class="section-num">02 · CITY-LEVEL PROGRAMS</div>
<h2 class="section-title">What cities are doing</h2>
<p class="section-note">Six city programs with real construction results, three of them already profiled elsewhere in this library for their zoning reforms.</p>
</div>
<div class="case-study-list">
<article class="card case-study" id="ahc-austin">
<div class="card-top">
<h3><a href="/cities/austin/">Austin, TX</a><span class="place">Texas</span></h3>
<span class="cs-tag">City program</span>
</div>
<div class="tagline">S.M.A.R.T. Housing · Affordability Unlocked, 2019</div>
<span class="field-label">Description</span>
<p>S.M.A.R.T. Housing waives permit and inspection fees for developments that are income-restricted, accessible, and near transit. Affordability Unlocked goes further, waiving height, parking, and setback standards outright for projects where at least half the units are income-restricted — no rezoning or hearing needed.</p>
<span class="field-label">Legislation</span>
<ul class="leg-list">
<li><a href="https://services.austintexas.gov/edims/document.cfm?id=111622">S.M.A.R.T. Housing Initiative — Ordinance No. 20071129-100</a> — waives permit, capital-recovery, and construction-inspection fees for developments certified as income-restricted, accessible, and near transit. The program began administratively in the 1990s; this 2007 ordinance codified it into city code.</li>
<li><a href="https://services.austintexas.gov/edims/document.cfm?id=319975">Affordability Unlocked — Ordinance No. 20190509-027</a> — lets income-restricted developments exceed base height limits and reduce parking and setback requirements in exchange for 40–99 years of affordability. Adopted May 2019.</li>
</ul>
<span class="field-label">Objective results</span>
<div class="stat-row">
<span class="stat"><b>~46,000</b> total units under Austin's density-bonus programs to date</span>
<span class="stat"><b>~13,000</b> of those affordable</span>
</div>
<span class="field-label">Subjective results</span>
<p class="muted">Affordability Unlocked's design — waiving standards outright rather than granting only a small bonus — appears to be what let it outperform S.M.A.R.T. Housing's fee waivers alone, and pairs naturally with the HOME zoning changes covered in Effective General Plan Reforms: a by-right zoning change plus an opt-in incentive that removes standards entirely reinforce each other.</p>
<span class="field-label">Public response</span>
<p class="muted">No rezoning or public hearing is required for qualifying projects under either program — by design, the approval process itself creates no forum for organized case-by-case opposition the way a rezoning hearing would. No independent survey of public reaction was located in sources reviewed; the program remains active with no documented rollback.</p>
<span class="field-label">Sources</span>
<div class="src-row"><a href="https://www.austintexas.gov/housing/development-incentives-and-agreements">City of Austin — development incentives ↗</a>
<a href="https://www.planetizen.com/news/2023/09/125695-affordability-unlocked-boosts-austins-affordable-housing-production">Planetizen, Affordability Unlocked results ↗</a></div>
</article>
<article class="card case-study" id="ahc-minneapolis">
<div class="card-top">
<h3><a href="/cities/minneapolis/">Minneapolis, MN</a><span class="place">Minnesota</span></h3>
<span class="cs-tag">City program</span>
</div>
<div class="tagline">4(d) Affordable Housing Incentive · tightened Nov 2024</div>
<span class="field-label">Description</span>
<p>A reduced property-tax classification for landlords who keep roughly a fifth of their units affordable and cap rent increases, in exchange for a ten-year commitment.</p>
<span class="field-label">Legislation</span>
<ul class="leg-list">
<li><a href="https://www.revisor.mn.gov/statutes/cite/273.13">Minn. Stat. §273.13, subd. 25(d) ("class 4d")</a> — the state property-tax classification giving a preferential rate to qualifying low-income rental housing certified by Minnesota Housing.</li>
<li><a href="https://www.minneapolismn.gov/business-services/business-assistance/financing-developers/4d-incentive/participation-rules/4d-incentive-guide">Minneapolis 4(d) Affordable Housing Incentive Program</a> — the city's own local participation terms and cash incentive layered on top of the state's class 4d classification. Administered as a city program rather than a numbered ordinance.</li>
</ul>
<span class="field-label">Objective results</span>
<div class="stat-row">
<span class="stat"><b>2,338</b> units enrolled in 2024 alone — 5x the historical average</span>
</div>
<span class="field-label">Subjective results</span>
<p class="muted">The initial qualifying-rent threshold may have been too generous relative to market rents in some submarkets: the city found many enrolled units were already renting near the qualifying price, meaning the tax break was not always buying new affordability.</p>
<span class="field-label">Public response</span>
<p class="muted">The city tightened the qualifying affordability threshold in late 2024 in direct response to its own internal review findings — a self-correction prompted by program data rather than by external opposition, litigation, or a ballot measure.</p>
<span class="field-label">Sources</span>
<div class="src-row"><a href="https://minneapolismn.gov/business-services/business-assistance/financing-developers/4d-incentive/participation-rules">Minneapolis — 4(d) participation rules ↗</a></div>
</article>
<article class="card case-study" id="ahc-seattle">
<div class="card-top">
<h3><a href="/cities/seattle/">Seattle, WA</a><span class="place">Washington</span></h3>
<span class="cs-tag">City program</span>
</div>
<div class="tagline">Multifamily Tax Exemption, since 1998</div>
<span class="field-label">Description</span>
<p>The nation's longest-running and most heavily used MFTE program, concentrated in the city's designated walkable urban centers.</p>
<span class="field-label">Legislation</span>
<ul class="leg-list">
<li><a href="https://www.seattle.gov/housing/housing-developers/multifamily-tax-exemption/mfte-program-7">Seattle MFTE Program 7</a> — the city's current reauthorization of its Multifamily Tax Exemption program, updating income/rent-restriction requirements and term lengths, effective November 2025.</li>
</ul>
<span class="field-label">Objective results</span>
<div class="stat-row">
<span class="stat"><b>33,956</b> total units since 1998, across 303 projects</span>
<span class="stat"><b>7,047</b> permanently income-restricted units</span>
<span class="stat"><b>1,950</b> units, 10 projects in 2025 alone — the top-producing city in the state</span>
</div>
<span class="field-label">Subjective results</span>
<p class="muted">A University of Washington evaluation found benefit-to-cost ratios of only 46–78% depending on program vintage, and roughly $35–80 million a year in foregone property tax shifted to other taxpayers — real unit production, but at a cost-effectiveness the city's own evaluators questioned.</p>
<span class="field-label">Public response</span>
<p class="muted">The city's October 2025 reforms — tighter income/rent limits and a $10,000-to-$300-per-unit fee increase — responded directly to that audit, and homebuilders publicly warned the changes could threaten the program's viability. The current program design is set to sunset in September 2029, meaning the political consensus behind MFTE's current form is unsettled rather than fixed.</p>
<span class="field-label">Sources</span>
<div class="src-row"><a href="https://seattle.gov/documents/Departments/Housing/ProgramsInitiatives/MFTEReauthorization/UW_MFTE_FinalReport_112224.pdf">UW / City of Seattle, MFTE program evaluation ↗</a>
<a href="https://www.hcmp.com/blog/hcmp-client-alert-seattle-city-council-passes-mfte-program-7">HCMP — Seattle Council passes MFTE Program 7 ↗</a>
<a href="https://www.theurbanist.org/homebuilders-warn-seattles-planned-mfte-changes-threaten-to-kill-affordability-program/">The Urbanist — homebuilders on the 2025 MFTE changes ↗</a></div>
</article>
<article class="card case-study" id="ahc-tacoma">
<div class="card-top">
<h3><a href="/cities/tacoma/">Tacoma, WA</a><span class="place">Washington</span></h3>
<span class="cs-tag">City program</span>
</div>
<div class="tagline">Multifamily Tax Exemption</div>
<span class="field-label">Description</span>
<p>Layered on top of the zoning changes already covered in this library's Home in Tacoma entry.</p>
<span class="field-label">Legislation</span>
<ul class="leg-list">
<li><a href="https://tacoma.gov/government/departments/community-and-economic-development/housing-division/tax-incentives/">Tacoma MFTE — Substitute Ordinance No. 29059</a> — governs the city's property-tax exemption for new multifamily housing in designated Residential Target Areas, with 8/12/20-year tiers tied to affordability. Passed October 2025.</li>
</ul>
<span class="field-label">Objective results</span>
<div class="stat-row">
<span class="stat"><b>550</b> units across 15 projects in 2025</span>
</div>
<span class="field-label">Subjective results</span>
<p class="muted">Tacoma led all Washington cities in MFTE project count in 2025 — a sign that the tax incentive and the Home in Tacoma zoning reform are reinforcing each other's effect rather than one masking the other.</p>
<span class="field-label">Public response</span>
<p class="muted">No independent public-reception study was located for Tacoma specifically; its project-count leadership among the 50+ Washington cities running MFTE programs in 2025 is the closest available signal of local traction.</p>
<span class="field-label">Sources</span>
<div class="src-row"><a href="https://tacoma.gov/government/departments/community-and-economic-development/housing-division/tax-incentives/">City of Tacoma — tax incentives ↗</a></div>
</article>
<article class="card case-study" id="ahc-spokane">
<div class="card-top">
<h3><a href="/cities/spokane/">Spokane, WA</a><span class="place">Washington</span></h3>
<span class="cs-tag">City program</span>
</div>
<div class="tagline">Multifamily Tax Exemption</div>
<span class="field-label">Description</span>
<p>A smaller but steady user of the same statewide tool, layered on top of Spokane's own middle-housing dimensional standards covered elsewhere in this library.</p>
<span class="field-label">Legislation</span>
<ul class="leg-list">
<li><a href="https://my.spokanecity.org/smc/?Chapter=08.15">Spokane Municipal Code Ch. 08.15</a> — establishes the city's local multifamily property-tax exemption program; most recently amended by Ordinance C36243 (2022).</li>
</ul>
<span class="field-label">Objective results</span>
<div class="stat-row">
<span class="stat"><b>226</b> units across 8 projects in 2025</span>
</div>
<span class="field-label">Subjective results</span>
<p class="muted">Consistent, modest output rather than a headline result on its own — useful mainly as a comparison point against Seattle's and Tacoma's larger-scale results under the identical state framework.</p>
<span class="field-label">Public response</span>
<p class="muted">No independent public-reception study was located; included here primarily for that comparison against the other two Washington cities in this library.</p>
<span class="field-label">Sources</span>
<div class="src-row"><a href="https://static.spokanecity.org/documents/economicdevelopment/incentives/mfte/2025-mfte-legislative-report.pdf">2025 statewide MFTE legislative report ↗</a></div>
</article>
<article class="card case-study" id="ahc-houston">
<div class="card-top">
<h3><a href="/cities/houston/">Houston, TX</a><span class="place">Texas</span></h3>
<span class="cs-tag">City program</span>
</div>
<div class="tagline">Minimum lot size reform, 1998 &amp; 2013</div>
<span class="field-label">Description</span>
<p>Houston has no conventional zoning code, but a 1998 rule allowing small lots (down to 1,400 sq ft) in place of larger open-space requirements, expanded citywide in 2013, unlocked one of the country's largest missing-middle infill booms.</p>
<span class="field-label">Legislation</span>
<ul class="leg-list">
<li><a href="https://www.huduser.gov/portal/periodicals/cityscape/vol26num3/ch9.pdf">1998 Chapter 42 amendment (inside I-610 Loop)</a> — allowed lots as small as 1,400 sq ft in place of the prior 5,000 sq ft minimum, in exchange for compensating open space. No single ordinance number is cited in any source reviewed, including Houston's own planning department materials.</li>
<li><a href="https://www.houstontx.gov/planning/AboutPD/news_releases13/chapter42.pdf">2013 Chapter 42 amendment (citywide)</a> — extended a version of the smaller-lot standard citywide through an opt-in Special Minimum Lot Size designation process, approved by City Council in April 2013.</li>
</ul>
<span class="field-label">Objective results</span>
<div class="stat-row">
<span class="stat"><b>20,000+</b> small-lot and townhome units by 2016, concentrated inside the inner loop</span>
<span class="stat"><b>~25,000</b> new residential parcels under 5,000 sq ft, 1999–2016</span>
<span class="stat"><b>27%</b> of new Inner Loop construction since 2005 is townhomes</span>
</div>
<span class="field-label">Subjective results</span>
<p class="muted">A density and production success story, not a walkability one — Houston's citywide Walk Score remains in car-dependent territory, and density in treated neighborhoods more than doubled 2000–2016 without producing a walkable, low-car neighborhood alongside it. Unlocking compact infill and building walkability are two different jobs.</p>
<span class="field-label">Public response</span>
<p class="muted">The reform was extended citywide in 2013 after its original 1998 version — limited to inside the I-610 Loop — held for 15 years without rollback, a rare deregulation that expanded in scope after implementation rather than being narrowed.</p>
<span class="field-label">Sources</span>
